<.page_title
  title="Skills"
  subtitle="Portable workflow guidance that keeps every attached agent operating inside ControlKeel's governed loop."
/>

<div class="mt-10 space-y-10">
  <section class="space-y-4">
    <.section_title>What skills are</.section_title>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      A ControlKeel skill is a portable instruction bundle in the <a
        href="https://agentskills.io/specification"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="text-primary underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
      >
        AgentSkills format
      </a>:
      a plain <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">SKILL.md</code>
      with a name, a description, and a governed workflow body — how to align on a goal, validate
      before an edit, bundle proof, or ship. Skills are how ControlKeel teaches an agent
      <em>how</em>
      to work under governance, while MCP tools and hooks give it the machinery to
      do so.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The agent never loads the full catalog. It sees each skill's description with the task at
      hand, and activates the one whose trigger conditions match. Once loaded, the skill's body
      steers the session — which CK calls to make, when to stop for a human, and what evidence to
      leave behind. Activation is deliberate: skills carry workflow discipline, not chat trivia.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-card border rounded-2xl shadow-card">
      <div class="grid gap-6 p-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="space-y-1.5">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
            Built-in
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
            Shipped with ControlKeel and always available, even on a fresh machine with no
            agent-specific installs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1.5">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
            User
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
            Installed under your home directory (<code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">~/.agents/skills</code>, <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">~/.codex/skills</code>, <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">~/.claude/skills</code>,
            and other host paths) so they travel with you across projects.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1.5">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
            Project
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
            Inside a governed repository (<code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">.agents/skills</code> and host peers). Project skills are only surfaced for trusted bindings, so one repo
            cannot inject instructions into another.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      When the same skill appears in several scopes with identical content, one definition wins —
      preferencing built-ins — and duplicate copies can be pruned automatically. This keeps a
      project install, a user install, and the built-in upgrade from fighting each other.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <.section_title>Available skills</.section_title>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      {length(@skill_catalog)} skills are visible to CK in this session. The built-in catalog
      ships with every install; user and project skills appear here when they are present. Each
      row's description is the trigger the agent weighs against its current task.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-card border rounded-2xl shadow-card overflow-clip">
      <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-border text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
          <tr>
            <th class="w-52 px-5 py-3 font-semibold">Skill</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold">Use when</th>
            <th class="w-32 px-5 py-3 font-semibold">Scope</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
          <%= for skill <- @skill_catalog do %>
            <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
              <td class="px-5 py-4 font-medium text-foreground font-mono text-xs">
                {skill.name}
              </td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4 leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
                {skill.description}
              </td>
              <td class="w-32 px-5 py-4 whitespace-nowrap">
                <span class="inline-block rounded-full bg-muted px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
                  {human_skill_scope(skill)}
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <.section_title>What you can do from the CLI</.section_title>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Every skill operation works from a terminal with
      <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">--project-root</code>
      selecting the governed project. Exports and installs write under
      <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
        controlkeel/dist/&lt;target&gt;
      </code>
      and record a manifest so
      <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">skills doctor</code>
      can audit what was placed, where, and when.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-card border rounded-2xl shadow-card overflow-clip">
      <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-border text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold">Command</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold">Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
          <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
            <td class="px-5 py-4">
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                controlkeel skills list
              </code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-5 py-4 text-muted-foreground">
              List the registry with diagnostics.
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">--json</code>
              for machine output;
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                --target &lt;target&gt;
              </code>
              filters by install target.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
            <td class="px-5 py-4">
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                controlkeel skills validate
              </code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-5 py-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Check every skill's frontmatter and structure; report warnings and errors with codes.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
            <td class="px-5 py-4">
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                controlkeel skills export --target &lt;target&gt;
              </code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-5 py-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Write a host-format skill bundle (targets include open-standard, claude-plugin,
              codex, github-repo, and host-native shapes). Defaults to open-standard.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
            <td class="px-5 py-4">
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                controlkeel skills install --target &lt;target&gt;
              </code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-5 py-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Install the bundle straight into the host's expected skills directory and report the
              destination.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
            <td class="px-5 py-4">
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                controlkeel token audit --mode skills
              </code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-5 py-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Measure the token cost of your skill surface. Modes: full, rules, skills, and tools.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="transition hover:bg-muted/30">
            <td class="px-5 py-4">
              <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
                controlkeel skills doctor
              </code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-5 py-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Diagnose the whole skills surface: catalog health, install manifests, attach
              clients, runtimes, and provider status.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <.section_title>How agents use skills</.section_title>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The moment a host is attached, CK writes skills to that host's native directory and
      registers them: a session-start hook surfaces the registry, and the host's skill mechanism
      makes each trigger available. Host commands come from
      <.link navigate="/docs/agents" class="text-primary underline-offset-2 hover:underline">
        the agents page
      </.link>
      —
      for example
      <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
        controlkeel attach opencode
      </code>
      prepares OpenCode skills plus MCP config, and the AgentSkills-standard
      <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
        npx skills add https://github.com/aryaminus/controlkeel --skill controlkeel-governance
      </code>
      flow reaches any skills.sh-compatible host.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground list-disc ml-5">
      <li>
        <strong>Trigger.</strong> The agent decides from the description; no one re-reads the
        whole catalog per task.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Load.</strong> The loaded body changes session behavior — align before building,
        validate before editing, bundle proof before shipping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Query over MCP.</strong>
        The same registry is exposed as typed tools: <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">ck_skill_list</code>, <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">ck_skill_load</code>, <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">ck_skill_validate</code>,
        and <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">ck_token_audit</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Manage over API.</strong>
        REST endpoints at
        <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">/api/v1/skills</code>
        list, fetch, export, install, and prune duplicates; see the developer portal.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Trust is scoped: project-level skills only load for a bound, trusted project — untrusted
      project directories are ignored, and
      <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-foreground">
        CONTROLKEEL_TRUST_PROJECT_SKILLS
      </code>
      flips opt-in scoping explicitly. A repo you just cloned cannot push instructions into your
      agent sessions until you trust it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <.section_title>Keep reading</.section_title>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground list-disc ml-5">
      <li>
        Getting started connects the first host: <.link
          navigate="/docs/getting-started"
          class="text-primary underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
        >
          install to first finding
        </.link>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The governance loop these skills operate inside: <.link
          navigate="/docs/governance"
          class="text-primary underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
        >
          how it governs
        </.link>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Host-by-host attach commands and what each agent gets: <.link
          navigate="/docs/agents"
          class="text-primary underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
        >
          how agents use CK
        </.link>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
